SEGMENT TREE (Range Sum Query)

This is a high-impact data structure — very useful for competitive programming, system design basics, and advanced DSA rounds.

1️⃣ Problem Statement

Given an integer array arr[], support the following operations efficiently:

Range Sum Query:
Find the sum of elements between indices L and R.

Update Query:
Update the value of an element at a given index.

👉 Both operations should run in O(log N) time.

2️⃣ Why Segment Tree?
Approach	Query Time
Brute Force	O(N) ❌
Prefix Sum	O(1) Query, O(N) Update ❌
Segment Tree	O(log N) both ✅
3️⃣ Key Idea (Intuition)

Divide array into segments

Each node stores sum of a segment

Root → entire array

Leaves → individual elements

4️⃣ Tree Representation

For array:

arr = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]


Segment Tree (conceptual):

            25
         /       \
       9          16
     /   \       /   \
    4     5     7     9
  /   \
 1     3

5️⃣ Algorithm
🔹 Build Tree

If l == r, store arr[l]

Else:

Split into left and right halves

Node value = sum of children

🔹 Range Sum Query

No overlap → return 0

Complete overlap → return node value

Partial overlap → query left + right

🔹 Update Element

Reach leaf node

Update value

Recalculate parent nodes

6️⃣ Java Code (Complete & Clean)
class SegmentTree {

    int[] tree;
    int n;

    // Constructor
    SegmentTree(int[] arr) {
        n = arr.length;
        tree = new int[4 * n];
        build(0, 0, n - 1, arr);
    }

    // Build the tree
    void build(int node, int start, int end, int[] arr) {
        if (start == end) {
            tree[node] = arr[start];
        } else {
            int mid = (start + end) / 2;
            build(2 * node + 1, start, mid, arr);
            build(2 * node + 2, mid + 1, end, arr);
            tree[node] =
                tree[2 * node + 1] + tree[2 * node + 2];
        }
    }

    // Range Sum Query
    int query(int node, int start, int end,
              int l, int r) {

        // No overlap
        if (r < start || l > end)
            return 0;

        // Complete overlap
        if (l <= start && end <= r)
            return tree[node];

        // Partial overlap
        int mid = (start + end) / 2;
        int leftSum = query(
            2 * node + 1, start, mid, l, r);
        int rightSum = query(
            2 * node + 2, mid + 1, end, l, r);

        return leftSum + rightSum;
    }

    // Update value at index
    void update(int node, int start, int end,
                int index, int value) {

        if (start == end) {
            tree[node] = value;
        } else {
            int mid = (start + end) / 2;

            if (index <= mid) {
                update(2 * node + 1,
                       start, mid, index, value);
            } else {
                update(2 * node + 2,
                       mid + 1, end, index, value);
            }

            tree[node] =
                tree[2 * node + 1] +
                tree[2 * node + 2];
        }
    }
}

7️⃣ Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 Tree Size
tree = new int[4 * n];


Safe size to store full tree

Prevents overflow

🔹 Build Function
if (start == end)


Leaf node

Stores array element

tree[node] = left + right;


Internal nodes store segment sum

🔹 Query Conditions
Case	Condition	Return
No overlap	outside range	0
Full overlap	inside range	tree[node]
Partial	split	left + right
🔹 Update Logic

Change leaf

Recursively update parents

Time = O(log N)

